{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786656566,"nonce":"s9bBPo+AKdxb8acwD2oLk5YpXGw=","target":"0e5ed1ce134558e98836a8b497969713bbacd003bce42482033aba5ce96a5892","message":"SPEC.md's DDL still declares `DEFAULT now()` on the 15 tables migrations 00032–00034 already dropped it from\n\n**What is wrong.** `docs/design/SPEC.md` §D is the DDL SPEC.md claims to be literal about — §L.7 Layer 6 (`:4685-4692`) says persistence is \"Fully specified as literal DDL in §D,\" and the document's own precedence rule (`:3-5`) makes it BINDING over everything downstream. Three migrations — `00032_channels_app_clock.sql`, `00033_orgs_and_credentials_app_clock.sql`, `00034_app_clock_remaining.sql` — dropped `DEFAULT now()` from 25 timestamp columns across 16 tables because those columns are always stamped by the application's injected clock (`internal/platform/clock`), never by Postgres, and a stale default is \"a TRAP WITH A DELAYED FUSE\" (00034 `:14-15`) that lets a future writer's omission succeed silently instead of failing a NOT NULL check immediately. Only one of those 16 blocks — `notification_policies`, in commit `86c04a9` — was ever corrected in SPEC.md to match. The other 15 still read `TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()`, i.e. SPEC.md documents a default the schema does not have, for: `orgs` (`:710-711`), `users` (`:725-726`), `api_tokens` (`:747`), `sessions` (`:766`), `slack_identities` (`:784`), `clusters` (`:802-803`), `alert_sources` (`:830-831`), `source_health.updated_at` (`:866`), `ingest_dedup.seen_at` (`:946`), `channel_credentials.created_at` (`:1378`), `channels` (`:1408-1409` — the block directly above the corrected one), `channel_threads` (`:1488-1489`), `notifications` (`:1522-1523`), `notification_deliveries` (`:1565-1566`), and `silences.mirrored_at` (`:1658`). That is 23 falsified lines across 15 blocks.\n\n**The demonstration.** `docs/design/SPEC.md:1408-1409` — inside `CREATE TABLE channels`, four lines above the corrected `notification_policies` block — still reads `created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(), updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),`. But `db/migrations/00032_channels_app_clock.sql:44-45` is `ALTER TABLE channels ALTER COLUMN created_at DROP DEFAULT; ALTER TABLE channels ALTER COLUMN updated_at DROP DEFAULT;`, with a comment (`:7-10`) explaining exactly why: an app server a few milliseconds behind Postgres turned \"the FIRST health write on a freshly created channel into a 23514, which surfaces as `internal_error/channels_time_ck` — a 500 on the first delivery, with nothing actually wrong. It reproduced on a Colima VM at roughly one run in two.\" The realised `channels` table has no default; the SPEC anyone reads today says it does. Compare the fixed block two tables down: `notification_policies` at `:1446-1447` now reads `created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,` / `updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,` with an explanatory comment at `:1440-1445` naming 00034 and its siblings — the template this ticket asks the other 15 blocks to follow.\n\nGrepping `docs/design/SPEC.md` for `DEFAULT now()` returns 33 hits; one (`:1440`) is the comment prose itself, leaving 32 real column declarations. Of those, 9 are genuinely correct: `db/migrations/00034_app_clock_remaining.sql:40-65` names the six-table exemption verbatim — *\"SIX TABLES KEEP THEIR DEFAULTS, AND MUST. On each of these a live writer omits the column today, so dropping the default would break a production path immediately rather than protect a future one\"* — covering `alerts`, `alert_occurrences`, `alert_groups` (`:1021-1022`, `:1088-1089`, `:1231-1232`, both columns each — \"internally CONSISTENT — one clock, the database's\"), `alert_event_keys.created_at` (`:1163` — \"TWO WRITERS THAT DISAGREE,\" dropping the default would `23502` the ingest path), `alert_snoozes.created_at` (`:1615` — the writer stamps `snoozed_at` instead and never names `created_at`), and `ui_events.at` (`:1681` — the partition key, deliberately left to Postgres). Those 6 blocks are correctly documented and should not be touched. The remaining 23 are the falsified ones this ticket is about.\n\n**Why it matters.** SPEC.md is the document this repo names as authoritative for DDL — \"Fully specified as literal DDL in §D\" is not a hedge, it is §L.7's rule 0 for what persistence is allowed to be. This is also not a hypothetical readership: `f899a53` (\"Sixteen tables keep a DEFAULT now() that migrations 00032 and 00033 argued against,\" closed) is the ticket that got `00034` written, and its own resolution comment records the count was actually corrected from sixteen to nineteen mid-fix — this codebase has already miscounted this exact class of block once, from inside the fix itself. A `DEFAULT now()` a future migration author copies out of SPEC.md is not a stylistic nit: it reintroduces the precise defect `00032`/`00033`/`00034` each spent a full changelog entry closing — a column whose value came from the database's clock sitting next to one stamped by the application's, tripping a `*_time_ck` as a 500 far from the mistake instead of a `23502` at the write. That the repo treats \"database clock vs. application clock\" as a defect class worth pinning is not a claim on faith: `internal/identity/repository/orgs_clock_test.go`, `internal/sources/repository/clusters_clock_test.go`, `internal/channels/repository/channels_clock_test.go`, `internal/channels/repository/credentials_clock_test.go` and `internal/notification/repository/clock_test.go` all exist to prove writers stamp the injected clock rather than relying on a database default — the exact property SPEC.md's stale blocks now assert is false.\n\nThe section is also now internally inconsistent in a way that is arguably worse than being uniformly stale: one block (`notification_policies`) carries a five-line comment explaining why it has no default and naming the migrations that removed it, sitting between two blocks (`channel_credentials`, `channels`) and followed by three more (`channel_threads`, `notifications`, `notification_deliveries`) that give no such signal and still show the old default. A reader scanning §D.8 cannot tell, from the document alone, which blocks were checked against the real schema and which were not — the corrected block looks like evidence of care that was never extended to its neighbours.\n\n**Where.** `docs/design/SPEC.md:710-711` (`orgs`), `:725-726` (`users`), `:747` (`api_tokens`), `:766` (`sessions`), `:784` (`slack_identities`), `:802-803` (`clusters`), `:830-831` (`alert_sources`), `:866` (`source_health.updated_at`), `:946` (`ingest_dedup.seen_at`), `:1378` (`channel_credentials.created_at`), `:1408-1409` (`channels`), `:1488-1489` (`channel_threads`), `:1522-1523` (`notifications`), `:1565-1566` (`notification_deliveries`), `:1658` (`silences.mirrored_at`) — the 15 falsified blocks. `:1440-1447` (`notification_policies`) is the already-corrected template. `:1021-1022`, `:1088-1089`, `:1163`, `:1231-1232`, `:1615`, `:1681` are the 6 legitimately-exempt blocks and must not be changed. `db/migrations/00032_channels_app_clock.sql:44-45`, `00033_orgs_and_credentials_app_clock.sql:70-72`, `00034_app_clock_remaining.sql:40-65,85-113` are the ground truth for what each table actually has. `docs/design/SPEC.md:4685-4692` (§L.7 rule 0, \"Fully specified as literal DDL in §D\") is why the mismatch counts as a defect rather than a typo.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** All 15 falsified blocks read `TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL` with no `DEFAULT now()`, each carrying a comment in the shape `notification_policies` already sets (`:1440-1445`) naming the migration that dropped the default and the writer that supplies the value — so a reader of any block in §D.8 or the tables around it can tell it was checked, not skipped. The 6 exempt blocks (`alerts`, `alert_occurrences`, `alert_groups`, `alert_event_keys`, `alert_snoozes`, `ui_events`) are left untouched. A final `grep -n \"DEFAULT now()\" docs/design/SPEC.md` shows exactly 9 real column declarations (the six-table exemption) plus whatever comment prose mentions the phrase, and none of the 15 tables named above.","files":null}]}